From 100996eb1d47fcdced922ee16a47072d4f2d6ea6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nekokabu <nekokabu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 03:45:23 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] n64: CPU instruction timing from hardware (cpu_timing
 v11, v14)

Two test ROMs time loops of VR4300 instructions with the COP0 count on NTSC and PAL
consoles (identical to the cycle). Against ares:

- The recompiler charged an instruction that took a slow path (cache miss, uncached
  access) one cycle twice: the slow path adds the cycles deferred up to the resume
  point, and the fast path flushed the same cycles again after it. Settle them before
  the resume label.
- The FPU is pipelined. add.s/sub.s/mul.s and their double forms issue in 2 cycles
  (ares: 3/5/8) and a dependent instruction waits until the result is ready 3 cycles
  after issue; conversions take 5; div.s and sqrt.s 29, sqrt.d 30 (ares: 58; div.d is
  assumed to match); a compare costs 1 (the recompiler charged 3). The recompiler
  schedules the dependency stalls per block (loads into FPU registers and stores/moves
  out of them included); the interpreter charges the issue cost.
- A data cache hit costs no extra cycle (ares charged 1 per load and store). What does
  cost a cycle: a load whose result the next instruction uses (not a branch), a store
  followed by another memory instruction, mfc0 of Count, mtc0, and the nullified delay
  slot of a likely branch that is not taken.
- An uncached RDRAM read stalls for 32 cycles (ares: 1-2); an RCP register read for 23
  (ares: 21).

With these the v11/v14 loops agree with the console to within 1-2 cycles per iteration,
except where the FPU's cost depends on operand values (add/sub with cancellation, a 0.0
dividend) and the 4-entry write buffer, which is not modelled (a burst of more than four
uncached stores costs ~16 cycles per extra store on the console).

